title: confirm-turn readback discards its run_tts result, so a TTS fallback there reaches no JSON and no transcript
updated_at: "2026-08-23T08:06:41Z"
---
Found by the B187/B177 review agent, 2026-08-23, and confirmed by the coordinator by reading the code.

src/hark/speak_then_listen/ask.py:146 calls
    speech_mod.run_tts(cfg, readback, provider=legs.tts, play=True)
for the "I heard: <text>. Say yes to send, or cancel." readback, and DISCARDS the return value.

Consequence: a B177 TTS fallback during the confirm readback is invisible to every surface an orchestrator actually reads. No `fallback` key, no `provider` in any payload, nothing in transcripts.jsonl. Only the HEP tts.provider_fallback event and the one stderr line show it.

Pre-existing, but it matters more now for two reasons:
1. B177 makes a mid-turn provider switch a normal occurrence rather than a never.
2. B186 transcripts.jsonl records tts_provider / tts_fallback_from and is meant to be trustworthy provenance. A readback that fell back contradicts that claim silently.

Fix: thread the readback result out so its `fallback` / `provider` reach the ask payload, and decide where it belongs in the JSON - the readback is a second TTS in the same turn, so it probably needs its own key rather than overwriting the question TTS block. That design question is why this was held out of B177 rather than bolted on.

Documented as a known gap in docs/PROVIDERS.md under the "A downgrade is never silent" section - keep the doc and the fix in sync when this lands.

B192 is a duplicate of this item, filed concurrently by the implementation agent on 2026-08-23 while the coordinator filed this one. B192 has been marked blocked-as-duplicate pointing here. docs/PROVIDERS.md references B191. Fold in anything unique to B192 before closing it.